 ('frz1', 'Harshavardhana came to the throne about 606 CE at Thanesar, after his brother was killed, and soon moved his capital to Kannauj — which from this point becomes the prize that northern dynasties fight over for the next two centuries, as the last episode of this unit will show.'),
 ('frz2', 'He spent his early reign campaigning, and brought much of the north under his authority.'),
 ('frz3', "And then he stopped. Moving south, he was checked at the Narmada by Pulakeshin the Second, the Chalukya ruler of Badami — an event recorded not by Harsha's own writers but in Pulakeshin's Aihole inscription, which is exactly why historians trust it."),
 ('frz4', 'The Narmada stayed the limit. It is a useful fact to carry: the north and the Deccan were separate political worlds, and the river is where they met.'),
 ('frz5', 'Hiuen Tsang describes an assembly at Kannauj and a great distribution at Prayag, where Harsha is said to have given away the contents of his treasury.'),
 ('frz6', 'Harsha patronised Buddhism and also supported other traditions, and he endowed the monastery at Nalanda, which Hiuen Tsang describes in detail as a great centre of learning. He was also a writer: three Sanskrit plays, including Nagananda and Ratnavali, are attributed to him.'),
 ('frz7', 'His administration looks less like the Mauryas and more like what came after. Officials were increasingly paid with grants of land rather than salary, and subordinate rulers — feudatories — held their territories while acknowledging him.'),
 ('frz8', 'That is why his empire dissolves so quickly when he dies without an heir, around 647 CE. An empire held together by personal authority over feudatories does not survive the person.'),
 ('plq1', 'How big was it really? The older view, following Bana and Hiuen Tsang, describes a large empire across northern India.'),
 ('plq2', 'A more sceptical reading notes that one of those sources was his employee and the other his guest, that the inscriptional evidence is thinner than the literary, and that what he held was overlordship over subordinate rulers rather than directly governed territory. The check at the Narmada is a fact; the extent north of it is argued about.'),
 ('ins1', 'Here is the memory tip, carved in stone. Two writers, one king — Bana at court, Hiuen Tsang travelling. Read them against each other.'),
 ('quiz1', 'Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. First: the court biography of Harsha, written by Banabhatta. The Harshacharita.'),
 ('quiz2', "Second: the river at which Pulakeshin the Second checked Harsha's advance south. The Narmada. Third: the great centre of learning that Harsha endowed and Hiuen Tsang described."),
